## 💎 3.2 A First Look at Variables & Strings
* Variable Definition: A named storage location in your computer's memory. You must declare a variable before it can be used to hold data.
* Syntax: `DataType VariableName;`
* Data Type Categories:
  * Primitive Types: Basic, built-in types defined by C# (e.g., strings and integers). They store fundamental/core types of data.
  * Non-Primitive Types: Collections (Arrays, Lists, Dictionaries), User-Defined Types (Classes, Structs, Enums, Interfaces), and Special Types (object, dynamic, var, delegate).
* Naming Rules: The first character must be a letter or an underscore (`_`). It cannot contain spaces or use C# keywords/reserved words.
* String Assignment: String values must be enclosed in a pair of double quotes:
  `productDescription = "Jamhuuriya University";`
  `productLabel.Text = productDescription;`
  `MessageBox.Show(productDescription);`
* String Concatenation: Appending one string to the end of another string using the `+` operator. It can happen between a string and another data type:
  `message = "Jamhuuriya" + "University"; // JamhuuriyaUniversity`
  `Value = 2025 + "Jamhuuriya Class";    // 2025Jamhuuriya Class`
* Local Variables and Scope:
  * Scope: Describes the part of a program in which a variable may be accessed. A local variable belongs to the method in which it was declared; only statements inside that method can see it.
  * Lifetime: The time period during which the variable exists in memory. It is created when the method starts executing and is destroyed when the method ends.
  * Duplicate Names: You cannot declare two variables with the same name within the same scope/method. However, you can have variables of the same name declared in different methods.
* Initialization Requirements: C# will not compile code that tries to use an unassigned variable. It throws the error: *"Use of unassigned local variable 'variableName'"*.
* Declaring Multiple Variables: You can declare multiple variables of the same type with one statement, split across multiple lines if needed:
  `string lastName, firstName, middleName;`

